I started with explorer mode which was quite simple once I obtained the Open AI API.
I simply set out to introduce the prompt and the appropriate black box, I worked with version 0.28 although they are continually updating the versions and changing the black boxes.



The hero mode was a little more complicated for me by introducing a new program and having to read all the documentation that was not updated on its own page.
I had to update python to version 3.11 by creating a new environment and installing all the dependencies.

The audio transcription is extremely accurate and even recording my own audio very quietly, the program was able to detect it.
You can include .wav, .mp3, and more audio formats.



The master mode was the most tedious because I had to use all the available technologies, at the same time it was the most interesting.

I used AWS because it is the cloud in which I have the most experience and in which scalability is easiest since it has lambda functions that are exceptionally good for what I was executing.

At first it was complicated because I wanted to save the audios that were uploaded in an S3 bucket but I realized that just having the lambda function executed when an API call is used could help.
For the REST API I used AWS API Gateway as well.
With this I managed to achieve the expected results by uploading an audio in the API call https://r3cv0ys8c7.execute-api.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/etapa/analysis.

To do this, the first thing I did was create a docker with the dependencies of my python script to later upload it to an AWS ECR and connect the lambda function so that it is executed with each API call.

This level has been the most complex since I do not have much experience in the subject but with full interest in improving everything quickly.

